          "title": "Section 4: University Lecture on Ancient Maritime Navigation",
          "transcript": "In today's lecture on maritime history, we explore the extraordinary navigation techniques developed by early Austronesian seafarers across the Pacific Ocean thousands of years before the invention of the magnetic compass. Navigators, known as wayfinders, relied on an intricate synthesis of celestial observations and oceanographic cues. By memorizing the rising and setting positions of over two hundred stars, they constructed a mental star compass. Furthermore, wayfinders could detect land hundreds of kilometers away by reading swell patterns—the subtle interference waves created when deep-ocean currents bounce off distant island chains. They also tracked biological indicators, observing the flight paths of pelagic birds such as noddies and terns returning to roost at dusk.",
